      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63065,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>The epidemiology depends on the etiology.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:25:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63066,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous metaplasia and cartilaginous metaplasia are due to irritation of the peritoneum in patients with peritoneal dialysis or pelvic disease {18753977; 12118117}. Keratin granulomas are caused by keratin debris&nbsp;/ non-viable squamous cells displaced from endometrial/ovarian endometrioid adenocarcinoma with squamous differentiation, atypical polypoid adenomyoma, cervical squamous cell carcinoma, or ruptured ovarian dermoid cyst {1698340}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:09:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63067,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:09:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63068,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Keratin granulomas and squamous metaplasia are usually 2&ndash;3&nbsp;mm nodules {1698340; 15186277}. Cartilaginous metaplasia may form nodules as large as 2.5&nbsp;cm {12118117; 11109160}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:09:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63069,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Keratin granulomas show laminated keratin or ghost squamous cells surrounded by foreign body&ndash;type giant cells, histiocytes, and inflammatory cells. Squamous metaplasia may have a diffuse or micronodular pattern; the former is associated with band-like subsurface fibrosis in peritoneal dialysis patients and the latter with inflammatory conditions {18753977}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T17:09:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
